---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import Endpoint from "../../components/docs/Endpoint.astro";
import Params from "../../components/docs/Params.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Tools" lead="Tools let your agent fetch live data from your own systems in the middle of a call, such as checking an order or reading a booking. You describe the function; Offhook calls your endpoint with the model's arguments and gives the answer back to the model." slug="tools">
        <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>You define a tool: a <code>name</code>, a <code>description</code> that tells the model <em>when</em> to use it, the arguments it takes, and an HTTP endpoint.</li>
          <li>You give the tool to an <a href="/docs/agents/">agent</a> through <code>tool_ids</code>.</li>
          <li>During a call, when the model decides it needs that data, it calls the tool. Offhook sends the request to your endpoint with the model's arguments and passes your JSON response back to the model, which carries on talking.</li>
        </ol>
        <Callout kind="note">
          The caller waits while your endpoint answers, so keep it fast. Each request is
          limited by the tool's <code>timeout_seconds</code> (at most 20). It helps to tell
          the agent, in its instructions, to say something like "one moment, let me check"
          before using a tool.
        </Callout>

        <h2 id="own-code">Your code, your servers</h2>
        <p>
          Offhook doesn't run your code; it calls it. To give the agent a database lookup,
          a calculation or another API, put that logic behind a small HTTP endpoint on your
          side and point a tool at it. Your code runs with your own dependencies and
          secrets, and Offhook only sends the arguments and reads the result.
        </p>
        <Code tabs={[{ label: "Your endpoint (Python)", lang: "text", code: `# Your own server: FastAPI, Flask, a Lambda, a Cloud Function, anything.
from fastapi import FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/tools/orders/{order_id}")
def check_order_status(order_id: str):
    order = db.query(Order).get(order_id)   # your code, your DB, your deps
    if order is None:
        return {"found": False}
    return {
        "found": True,
        "status": order.status,
        "items": order.item_count,
        "delivery_date": str(order.delivery_date),
    }` }, { label: "Register it", lang: "bash", code: `curl -X POST http://localhost:8000/v1/tools \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OFFHOOK_KEY" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "name": "check_order_status",
    "description": "Look up an order by ID; returns status, items, and delivery date.",
    "method": "GET",
    "url": "https://api.yourapp.com/tools/orders/{order_id}",
    "parameters": {
      "properties": { "order_id": { "type": "string" } },
      "required": ["order_id"]
    },
    "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer your-internal-key" }
  }'` }]} />
        <p>Two rules for the endpoint:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>It must be public HTTPS.</strong> Requests to private, loopback and cloud-metadata addresses are blocked. During local development, expose your endpoint with a tunnel such as ngrok and use that URL.</li>
          <li><strong>It must answer within <code>timeout_seconds</code>.</strong> For slow work, start it in the background and return straight away, then report the result later, for example through a <a href="/docs/webhooks/">webhook</a>.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="create">Create a tool</h2>
        <Endpoint method="POST" path="/v1/tools" />
        <Params rows={[
          { name: "name", type: "string", required: true, desc: "The function name the model calls: letters, digits and underscores, e.g. <code>check_order_status</code>. Must be unique within your tenant. <code>record_result</code> and <code>end_call</code> are reserved." },
          { name: "description", type: "string", required: true, desc: "What the tool does and when to use it. This is what the model reads to decide whether to call it." },
          { name: "parameters", type: "object", desc: "The arguments, in JSON Schema style: <code>{ \"properties\": { … }, \"required\": [ … ] }</code>." },
          { name: "method", type: "string", desc: "<code>GET</code> (the default) or <code>POST</code>." },
          { name: "url", type: "string", required: true, desc: "Your HTTPS endpoint. Write <code>{name}</code> in the path to insert an argument there." },
          { name: "headers", type: "object", desc: "Fixed headers, such as <code>Authorization</code>. Stored encrypted and never returned." },
          { name: "timeout_seconds", type: "int", desc: "1 to 20. The default is 10." },
        ]} />
        <Code tabs={[{ label: "cURL", lang: "bash", code: `curl -X POST http://localhost:8000/v1/tools \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OFFHOOK_KEY" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "name": "check_order_status",
    "description": "Look up an order by its ID and return its status, items, and delivery date.",
    "method": "GET",
    "url": "https://api.yourapp.com/orders/{order_id}",
    "parameters": {
      "properties": {
        "order_id": { "type": "string", "description": "The order ID, e.g. ORD-1042" }
      },
      "required": ["order_id"]
    },
    "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer sk_live_…" },
    "timeout_seconds": 8
  }'` }, { label: "Response", lang: "json", code: `{
  "id": "c4e81b2f-9a3d-4c6e-8f1b-7d2a5e9c3b10",
  "name": "check_order_status",
  "description": "Look up an order by its ID and return its status, items, and delivery date.",
  "method": "GET",
  "url": "https://api.yourapp.com/orders/{order_id}",
  "parameters": { "properties": { "order_id": { "type": "string" } }, "required": ["order_id"] },
  "has_auth": true,
  "timeout_seconds": 8,
  "created_at": "2026-10-15T10:00:00Z"
}` }]} />

        <h2 id="mapping">How arguments become a request</h2>
        <p>The model's arguments only ever fill in the request. They can't change the host, scheme, method or headers.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Path:</strong> each <code>{'{name}'}</code> in the URL is replaced with that argument, URL-encoded.</li>
          <li><strong>GET:</strong> the other arguments become the query string.</li>
          <li><strong>POST:</strong> the other arguments become the JSON body.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>So <code>check_order_status(order_id="ORD-1042")</code> sends <code>GET https://api.yourapp.com/orders/ORD-1042</code> with your <code>Authorization</code> header.</p>

        <h2 id="attach">Give a tool to an agent</h2>
        <p>Pass <code>tool_ids</code> when you create the agent. It can then use any of those tools during a call.</p>
        <Code tabs={[{ label: "cURL", lang: "bash", code: `curl -X POST http://localhost:8000/v1/agents \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OFFHOOK_KEY" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "name": "Order support",
    "instructions": "Help the caller with questions about their order. Use check_order_status to look up specifics.",
    "stt_connection_id": "<stt-connection-id>",
    "tts_connection_id": "<tts-connection-id>",
    "llm_connection_id": "<llm-connection-id>",
    "tool_ids": ["c4e81b2f-9a3d-4c6e-8f1b-7d2a5e9c3b10"]
  }'` }]} />

        <h2 id="list">List, retrieve, update and delete</h2>
        <Endpoint method="GET" path="/v1/tools" />
        <Endpoint method="GET" path="/v1/tools/{id}" />
        <Endpoint method="PATCH" path="/v1/tools/{id}" />
        <Endpoint method="DELETE" path="/v1/tools/{id}" />
        <p>Header values are never returned. <code>has_auth</code> tells you whether any are set.</p>

        <h2 id="result">What the model gets back</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Success:</strong> <code>{'{ "ok": true, "result": <your JSON> }'}</code>. Very large responses are shortened.</li>
          <li><strong>Your endpoint returned an error status:</strong> <code>{'{ "error": "endpoint returned HTTP 404", "body": … }'}</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Timeout, blocked URL or missing argument:</strong> <code>{'{ "error": "…" }'}</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In every error case the call carries on, so the agent can apologise and move on.</p>

        <Callout kind="warn">
          Treat whatever your endpoint returns as something the agent might say out loud.
          Don't return secrets or data the caller shouldn't hear.
        </Callout>

</DocsLayout>
